# Regression: the HALT fast-forward must never wedge on a stuck deadline.
# halt_masked_ula_int.nex MASKS the ULA frame interrupt (NR $22 bit 2) while
# ARMING the line interrupt (NR $22 bit 1 + NR $23), then `ei : halt`s. The CPU
# halts waiting on the line int (a future wake source) while the masked ULA
# frame-int deadline sits at the current cycle. Before the fix, the fast-forward
# snapped to that stuck deadline (Skipped=0) and spun forever — no --cycles
# budget could bound it, since emulated time never advanced. The fixture keeps the line interrupt active while masking the ULA interrupt.
#
# The fix: (1) a masked ULA frame int still advances its deadline every frame
# (the ULA pulses regardless of whether the CPU takes it), and (2) the HALT
# fast-forward only snaps to strictly-future deadlines. Either layer alone breaks
# the wedge; together the run reaches its frame budget.
#
# timeout_s makes a REGRESSION fail (subprocess killed) instead of hanging the
# whole suite. Source: data/nex/src/halt_masked_ula_int.asm.

description    = HALT fast-forward does not livelock on a masked ULA-int deadline
nex            = data/nex/halt_masked_ula_int.nex
machine        = next50
cycles         = 2000000
no_ay          = true
timeout_s      = 30

expect_exit              = 0
expect_status_field_eq   = exit.result=BudgetReached
